                <pb facs="0130" n="50" sameAs="frus:frus1901/pg50"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Subinclosure 3.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">State Department to United States Minister.—Letter
                        dated October 18, 1896.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg></opener>
                <p>As there is no record showing that any concession was ever actually made to the
                    United States, and in view of the further fact that we, many years ago,
                    relinquished whatever control we may have been allowed to exercise over the
                    land, it would seem that we are not in a position to maintain that we are
                    entitled to resume jurisdiction over the tract, even if it is considered
                    desirable to do so.</p>
                <p>The preceding remarks are simply intended to give you the Department’s
                    understanding of this case, and it is not necessary that you should do anything
                    in the matter, at least while awaiting the reply of the Chinese Government to
                    your protest and until you ascertain what the attitude of that Government will
                    be in the matter.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
